#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4beb4dd7-21e3-5447-b949-ef6f23b5e291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293439} {:answer/id #uuid "1c99d03c-1ff4-5a10-adaa-70bcc44e772d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293440} {:answer/id #uuid "338acf0c-7949-50e9-a083-803a4948a091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293441} {:answer/id #uuid "17f69769-9fb8-5130-95f3-bb7a3aad5ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293442} {:answer/id #uuid "37c3f0c6-95bb-5f64-b213-7c57150e4518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f69769-9fb8-5130-95f3-bb7a3aad5ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293442} :answer-field/id #uuid "23997d42-5193-5915-a21f-f7d0b34cc6eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c5260bf-7853-55b2-aacc-c274f42999cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106405" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d1de36-c45f-576e-a7f1-ddd4556e6e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos \\theta , y = 49\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149077} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e6c06b-fbd2-58df-8e45-287b8f1bd672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\sin \\theta , y = 7\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149078} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f202dd-0ca4-50e2-93ae-12d42b873745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 7\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149079} {:answer/id #uuid "bebafa99-24a7-5707-8536-24d126c9d58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 7\\sin \\theta , y = 2\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149080} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a2b8e-346c-559b-aa35-5c942ebd9ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos \\theta , y = 7\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f202dd-0ca4-50e2-93ae-12d42b873745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 7\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149079} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d808a2-555f-5182-9d76-592cf8cf6ea8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149076}] :question/id #uuid "46c88046-0f62-50bb-8a31-ae13a6c535ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-15856" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-15856.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7, 0)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8926e406-17d6-57f1-966b-8928bba30af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151261} {:answer/id #uuid "e82af90d-3c6f-5949-be81-cd6a2cc4ed4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x - 1)^{2})/(16) + (y + 1)^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151262} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb96178-2971-521d-b5ae-5ee963f0a01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x - 1)^{2})/(4) + (y + 1)^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151263} {:answer/id #uuid "1694221d-2670-5f72-9589-0944eb02a1e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x + 1)^{2})/(16) + (y - 1)^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151264} {:answer/id #uuid "3422be96-1eb0-5657-b2e9-aa9a9befe42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x + 1)^{2})/(16) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(4) = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1694221d-2670-5f72-9589-0944eb02a1e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((x + 1)^{2})/(16) + (y - 1)^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151264} :answer-field/id #uuid "04613534-12f9-512e-99a3-b990601cf341" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151260}] :question/id #uuid "ddcf2b55-d913-5f98-8f2e-d38863acbf2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79375" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$x = 4\\cos \\theta  - 1, y = \\sin \\theta  + 1, 0 \\le \\theta  < 2\\pi $."}]}
